#  tm:begin gitattributes
# Generated views of the control DB. A "conflict" in one of these carries no information the
# DB does not already hold, and a line-level merge of them is always wrong: keep whichever
# side wins, then `tm render` regenerates the byte-for-byte truth.
#
# `merge=ours` is NOT a built-in driver — it names one that must be registered locally, once
# per clone, because git refuses to let a repo ship executable merge-driver commands through
# versioned files. Without it git still stops for manual resolution on these paths despite
# this block. Worktrees share the parent's config, so one run covers all of them:
#
#     git config merge.ours.driver true
#
# `tm sync` still never runs that command itself (§10.5 rule 7) — but a clone missing it
# now makes every `tm sync` end loudly and non-zero, instead of silently reporting the
# clone as current, until it's registered. See T6.9, T39.21.
program/BOARD.md merge=ours
program/LOG.md merge=ours
program/INBOX.md merge=ours
program/mission-control.json merge=ours
#  tm:end gitattributes
